Simple coryza manifesting as transient nasal congestion and mild rhinorrhea
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Nasal congestion, Clear to white rhinorrhea, Transient scratchy pharynx

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-037
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of upper respiratory symptoms requiring assessment to rule out influenza, COVID-19, or acute bacterial sinusitis in the context of recent community outbreaks.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted sudden nasal congestion, malaise, and a scratchy throat. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to auscultate the lungs, rule out lower respiratory tract involvement or severe systemic viral illness (like influenza), and provide appropriate public health guidance.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My head is completely stuffed up, my nose won't stop running, and I feel generally rundown.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a gradual onset of bilateral nasal mucosal congestion over the past 24-48 hours. Describes mild clear to white anterior rhinorrhea and a transient scratchy sensation in the posterior pharynx. Notes a low-grade feeling of general malaise and intermittent mild sneezing without paroxysms. Complete absence of high fever, facial pain, or maxillary toothache. Normal body temperature or isolated mild subjective chill.
Functional Status: Fully independent, continuing light duties at home.
Social/Safety Concerns: Potential transmission to household members.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/78 | HR: 76 | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, frequently blowing nose, sounding congested.
Physical Exam: Anterior rhinoscopy reveals erythematous nasal mucosa with clear secretions. Diagnostic triad confirmed: nasal congestion, clear to white rhinorrhea, and transient scratchy pharynx. Oropharynx shows mild erythema without exudate; tonsils are not enlarged. Pulmonary auscultation reveals clear bilateral lung fields without wheeze or crackles. Absence of acute sinus tenderness to percussion. Normal tympanic membranes bilaterally.
Environmental Observations: Tissues accumulated near the bedside.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking over-the-counter paracetamol as needed.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Simple coryza manifesting as transient nasal congestion and mild rhinorrhea (Common Cold) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Bacterial Sinusitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Short duration of symptoms (<7 days), clear/white discharge rather than purulent, and absence of focal facial pain.

Influenza - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Gradual onset, absence of high fever (>38.5C), and lack of severe, debilitating myalgia and systemic prostration.

Lower Respiratory Tract Infection (Bronchitis/Pneumonia) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear lung fields on auscultation, normal respiratory rate, and normal oxygen saturation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute upper respiratory tract symptoms that required physical assessment to confidently exclude more severe infectious etiologies, particularly early pneumonia or influenza. Delaying the assessment carried a risk of missing lower airway compromise or delaying potential antiviral interventions if influenza was suspected. The home evaluation confirmed a strictly localized viral upper respiratory infection, safely averting an unnecessary ED visit and preventing inappropriate antibiotic prescribing.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance regarding the viral etiology. Recommendation for supportive care with oral hydration, rest, and simple analgesia (paracetamol/ibuprofen) for malaise. Use of saline nasal sprays to relieve congestion.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement strict hand hygiene, washing hands with soap for 20 seconds immediately after blowing the nose or before touching shared household surfaces, to physically break the chain of viral transmission to others.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if symptoms fail to improve after 10 days or acutely worsen.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop sudden shortness of breath, severe chest pain, coughing up blood, or extreme lethargy and confusion.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a clinical viral syndrome; symptoms can fluctuate and secondary bacterial infections occasionally develop.
Clinical Course: The cold is expected to peak at days 3-5 and spontaneously resolve within seven to ten days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a high fever above 39C, if your nasal discharge becomes thick and bloody with severe facial pain, or if you feel breathless while resting.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A common cold virus has infected the lining of your nose and throat. Antibiotics do not kill viruses. Your immune system will clear the virus naturally over the next week.
Actionable Steps: Rest and drink plenty of water. Use a saline nasal spray for congestion. Do not ask for antibiotics. Wash your hands frequently.

